1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,704
ZERO just got his first credit card.

2
00:00:03,004 --> 00:00:04,876
This is its first coffee.

3
00:00:10,900 --> 00:00:13,324
That café has never met ZERO.

4
00:00:13,624 --> 00:00:15,832
His bank has never met the café.

5
00:00:16,032 --> 00:00:17,832
So who decides to trust him?

6
00:00:28,232 --> 00:00:30,810
Between this tap and that cup, a tiny

7
00:00:30,810 --> 00:00:33,248
message will cross three companies,

8
00:00:33,548 --> 00:00:35,420
survive a fraud test,

9
00:00:35,520 --> 00:00:37,848
and come back with a yes or a no.

10
00:00:37,948 --> 00:00:39,820
ZERO is riding it.

11
00:00:40,820 --> 00:00:42,692
First surprise:

12
00:00:42,992 --> 00:00:44,840
that card has no battery.

13
00:00:44,990 --> 00:00:47,310
The terminal's radio field wakes up

14
00:00:47,310 --> 00:00:49,166
its chip for a split second,

15
00:00:49,566 --> 00:00:51,558
just long enough to say one thing.

16
00:00:52,258 --> 00:00:54,130
To ZERO, it's his money.

17
00:00:54,980 --> 00:00:56,924
To the system, it's this.

18
00:00:57,224 --> 00:00:59,096
A card number.

19
00:00:59,246 --> 00:01:01,118
An amount.

20
00:01:01,218 --> 00:01:03,090
Which shop.

21
00:01:03,190 --> 00:01:05,374
And a code that only works once.

22
00:01:07,074 --> 00:01:08,370
Copy that code,

23
00:01:08,670 --> 00:01:10,590
and it's useless a second later.

24
00:01:10,790 --> 00:01:12,419
That's why a chip card is

25
00:01:12,419 --> 00:01:13,526
so hard to clone.

26
00:01:14,376 --> 00:01:16,008
Pay with a phone instead,

27
00:01:16,308 --> 00:01:18,468
and even the card number is fake.

28
00:01:18,568 --> 00:01:20,512
It's a stand-in called a token.

29
00:01:20,812 --> 00:01:23,141
Visa says it has handed out more than

30
00:01:23,141 --> 00:01:24,652
sixteen billion of them.

31
00:01:25,502 --> 00:01:26,865
ZERO thinks it's going

32
00:01:26,865 --> 00:01:28,166
straight to his bank.

33
00:01:28,466 --> 00:01:30,338
It isn't.

34
00:01:30,738 --> 00:01:32,106
Not even close.

35
00:01:34,656 --> 00:01:36,528
First stop:

36
00:01:36,828 --> 00:01:38,873
a company the coffee shop pays

37
00:01:38,873 --> 00:01:40,236
to handle its cards.

38
00:01:40,386 --> 00:01:43,026
It works for the shop, not for ZERO.

39
00:01:43,426 --> 00:01:45,682
And it has no idea who he is.

40
00:01:46,532 --> 00:01:48,457
It only reads the first digits

41
00:01:48,457 --> 00:01:49,676
of the card number.

42
00:01:49,976 --> 00:01:52,016
They say which network to ask,

43
00:01:52,416 --> 00:01:54,624
and which bank is behind the card.

44
00:01:56,124 --> 00:01:57,996
Which raises a better question.

45
00:01:58,296 --> 00:02:00,528
Why does a coffee need a network at all?

46
00:02:02,224 --> 00:02:04,840
Because thousands of banks issue cards,

47
00:02:05,140 --> 00:02:07,204
and millions of shops take them.

48
00:02:07,354 --> 00:02:09,226
Most of them have never met.

49
00:02:09,626 --> 00:02:11,328
The network is where they

50
00:02:11,328 --> 00:02:12,554
all agree to meet,

51
00:02:12,954 --> 00:02:14,826
under one set of rules.

52
00:02:15,476 --> 00:02:17,611
In the U.S., that's mostly

53
00:02:17,611 --> 00:02:19,172
Visa or Mastercard.

54
00:02:19,472 --> 00:02:22,019
In a single year, Visa alone

55
00:02:22,019 --> 00:02:24,656
handled 258 billion payments.

56
00:02:25,056 --> 00:02:27,624
That's about eight thousand every second.

57
00:02:29,024 --> 00:02:30,898
All of it runs through four

58
00:02:30,898 --> 00:02:32,216
giant data centers,

59
00:02:32,516 --> 00:02:34,388
with spare power,

60
00:02:34,638 --> 00:02:36,510
spare cooling

61
00:02:36,660 --> 00:02:38,196
and spare connections,

62
00:02:38,346 --> 00:02:40,578
so a question never has to wait.

63
00:02:44,928 --> 00:02:46,800
Here's the strange part.

64
00:02:47,100 --> 00:02:49,284
The network doesn't move money either.

65
00:02:49,484 --> 00:02:51,356
It moves questions.

66
00:02:52,756 --> 00:02:55,036
Last stop: ZERO's bank.

67
00:02:55,336 --> 00:02:57,088
The one that gave him the card.

68
00:02:57,288 --> 00:02:59,209
It has a fraction of a second

69
00:02:59,209 --> 00:03:00,600
to answer two things.

70
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:02,872
Does he have enough credit?

71
00:03:03,272 --> 00:03:05,144
And is this really him?

72
00:03:05,894 --> 00:03:08,678
For the second one, it doesn't ask ZERO.

73
00:03:08,978 --> 00:03:10,850
It asks his habits.

74
00:03:11,150 --> 00:03:13,994
Visa says its own system weighs more than

75
00:03:13,994 --> 00:03:16,838
five hundred details about every payment,

76
00:03:17,238 --> 00:03:18,870
in about a millisecond.

77
00:03:19,420 --> 00:03:21,628
But ZERO's card is brand new.

78
00:03:21,928 --> 00:03:23,440
He has no habits.

79
00:03:23,740 --> 00:03:25,612
And to a fraud machine,

80
00:03:26,012 --> 00:03:27,621
no history can look a lot

81
00:03:27,621 --> 00:03:28,844
like a stolen card.

82
00:03:30,120 --> 00:03:31,776
Back in the coffee shop,

83
00:03:32,076 --> 00:03:34,572
the clock says one point one seconds.

84
00:03:35,122 --> 00:03:36,997
If a bank ever takes too

85
00:03:36,997 --> 00:03:38,482
long, or goes down,

86
00:03:38,782 --> 00:03:40,654
the network can answer for it,

87
00:03:40,754 --> 00:03:42,626
using AI to stand in.

88
00:03:42,726 --> 00:03:44,814
But ZERO's bank isn't down.

89
00:03:45,314 --> 00:03:46,658
It's just not sure.

90
00:03:47,408 --> 00:03:49,280
Then the evidence adds up.

91
00:03:49,580 --> 00:03:51,452
A real chip,

92
00:03:51,852 --> 00:03:54,084
one only the real card can make.

93
00:03:54,184 --> 00:03:56,056
A tiny amount.

94
00:03:56,456 --> 00:03:58,125
A coffee shop a few blocks

95
00:03:58,125 --> 00:03:59,216
from his address.

96
00:04:00,166 --> 00:04:02,038
Credit: yes.

97
00:04:02,338 --> 00:04:03,731
And now look at what the

98
00:04:03,731 --> 00:04:04,834
bank actually does.

99
00:04:05,234 --> 00:04:07,106
It doesn't send any money.

100
00:04:07,406 --> 00:04:09,808
It puts a hold on four seventy-five

101
00:04:09,808 --> 00:04:11,318
of ZERO's credit line.

102
00:04:11,618 --> 00:04:13,490
A promise.

103
00:04:14,890 --> 00:04:17,050
And the promise works both ways.

104
00:04:17,350 --> 00:04:19,150
If the coffee never showed up,

105
00:04:19,550 --> 00:04:21,590
ZERO could dispute the charge,

106
00:04:21,690 --> 00:04:23,227
and the money would be pulled

107
00:04:23,227 --> 00:04:24,234
back from the shop.

108
00:04:25,390 --> 00:04:27,598
The answer rides back the same way.

109
00:04:27,898 --> 00:04:28,714
Bank,

110
00:04:29,014 --> 00:04:30,886
network,

111
00:04:31,086 --> 00:04:32,382
the shop's company,

112
00:04:32,582 --> 00:04:33,662
the terminal.

113
00:04:34,962 --> 00:04:36,834
Two seconds.

114
00:04:38,662 --> 00:04:40,534
ZERO thinks he just paid.

115
00:04:40,834 --> 00:04:42,706
He didn't.

116
00:04:43,206 --> 00:04:45,150
Nobody has paid anybody.

117
00:04:45,650 --> 00:04:47,522
Not yet.

118
00:04:47,822 --> 00:04:50,078
So when does the money actually move?

119
00:04:52,178 --> 00:04:54,386
ZERO decides to wait and see.

120
00:04:54,686 --> 00:04:56,899
Coffee after coffee, the terminal

121
00:04:56,899 --> 00:04:58,910
collects every yes of the day.

122
00:04:59,310 --> 00:05:01,761
By nine PM, this one little machine is

123
00:05:01,761 --> 00:05:04,470
holding three hundred and twelve promises.

124
00:05:06,270 --> 00:05:08,299
At closing time, it sends

125
00:05:08,299 --> 00:05:09,678
them all at once,

126
00:05:09,978 --> 00:05:11,850
in one bundle.

127
00:05:12,550 --> 00:05:14,125
Tonight, the network does

128
00:05:14,125 --> 00:05:16,078
something completely different.

129
00:05:16,378 --> 00:05:18,250
It adds up who owes whom.

130
00:05:18,650 --> 00:05:20,996
Every bank's millions of coffees,

131
00:05:20,996 --> 00:05:23,696
groceries and plane tickets become one

132
00:05:23,696 --> 00:05:24,194
number.

133
00:05:24,494 --> 00:05:26,224
And each number is settled

134
00:05:26,224 --> 00:05:27,422
with one transfer,

135
00:05:27,922 --> 00:05:29,794
once a business day.

136
00:05:30,544 --> 00:05:33,472
And then, finally, the money moves.

137
00:05:33,772 --> 00:05:35,980
Not four seventy-five at a time.

138
00:05:36,380 --> 00:05:38,924
In huge lumps, between banks.

139
00:05:39,124 --> 00:05:41,720
Within a day or two, the coffee shop's

140
00:05:41,720 --> 00:05:43,564
money lands in its account.

141
00:05:44,714 --> 00:05:46,663
Every card payment in America

142
00:05:46,663 --> 00:05:47,738
works like this.

143
00:05:48,038 --> 00:05:50,102
A two-second promise in the day.

144
00:05:50,602 --> 00:05:53,098
A giant settling of accounts at night.

145
00:05:54,598 --> 00:05:56,274
And every one of them leaves a

146
00:05:56,274 --> 00:05:57,670
little behind on the way.

147
00:05:57,970 --> 00:05:59,799
The coffee shop doesn't get

148
00:05:59,799 --> 00:06:01,018
four seventy-five.

149
00:06:01,868 --> 00:06:04,820
The biggest slice goes to ZERO's bank:

150
00:06:05,120 --> 00:06:07,496
on average, around two percent of

151
00:06:07,496 --> 00:06:09,440
every credit card purchase.

152
00:06:09,540 --> 00:06:11,412
The network takes a slice.

153
00:06:11,812 --> 00:06:14,356
The shop's card company takes a slice.

154
00:06:14,556 --> 00:06:16,900
On ZERO's coffee, that's maybe

155
00:06:16,900 --> 00:06:18,540
ten or fifteen cents.

156
00:06:20,040 --> 00:06:23,726
Across the country, shops paid more than

157
00:06:23,726 --> 00:06:27,504
187 billion dollars in card fees in 2024.

158
00:06:29,004 --> 00:06:31,040
Pay with a debit card from a big

159
00:06:31,040 --> 00:06:32,884
bank, and the bank's slice is

160
00:06:32,884 --> 00:06:34,284
capped by federal law.

161
00:06:34,584 --> 00:06:36,480
Credit cards have no cap.

162
00:06:36,880 --> 00:06:38,862
And the fancier the card, the

163
00:06:38,862 --> 00:06:40,024
bigger the slice.

164
00:06:41,424 --> 00:06:43,296
So who really pays that?

165
00:06:43,596 --> 00:06:46,452
The coffee shop builds it into its prices.

166
00:06:46,852 --> 00:06:47,956
For everyone.

167
00:06:48,256 --> 00:06:50,560
Including people who pay in cash.

168
00:06:51,760 --> 00:06:54,366
Economists estimate that cash and debit

169
00:06:54,366 --> 00:06:56,771
card users hand about thirty billion

170
00:06:56,771 --> 00:06:59,176
dollars a year to credit card users.

171
00:06:59,476 --> 00:07:01,794
That's what pays for the points, the

172
00:07:01,794 --> 00:07:03,340
miles and the cash back.

173
00:07:04,840 --> 00:07:05,896
And ZERO?

174
00:07:06,196 --> 00:07:08,428
In a few weeks, he gets a bill.

175
00:07:09,840 --> 00:07:12,792
If he pays it in full, the loan was free.

176
00:07:13,092 --> 00:07:15,076
If he doesn't, the average rate on

177
00:07:15,076 --> 00:07:17,119
cards that charge interest is about

178
00:07:17,119 --> 00:07:18,636
twenty-two percent a year.

179
00:07:19,136 --> 00:07:21,296
That's the other way the bank gets paid.

180
00:07:21,796 --> 00:07:24,793
Right now, Americans owe one point two six

181
00:07:24,793 --> 00:07:27,076
trillion dollars on their cards.

182
00:07:28,876 --> 00:07:30,748
Next morning.

183
00:07:31,048 --> 00:07:32,920
Eight fourteen.

184
00:07:33,670 --> 00:07:35,542
Two seconds.

185
00:07:35,842 --> 00:07:37,090
Three companies.

186
00:07:37,390 --> 00:07:39,048
A machine that's starting to

187
00:07:39,048 --> 00:07:40,054
learn his habits.

188
00:07:40,354 --> 00:07:42,226
And not one cent moving.

189
00:07:52,026 --> 00:07:53,802
And the bank that just lent him

190
00:07:53,802 --> 00:07:54,834
four seventy-five?

191
00:07:55,134 --> 00:07:57,414
It's quietly hoping he pays late.

192
00:07:57,914 --> 00:07:59,474
That's a whole other system.
